  "id": "DrugBank_DB06650 - MONDO_grouped_4948_872_4963_20511_4947_814",
  "verbalization": "Ofatumumab, a monoclonal antibody, is implicated in the treatment of Richter Syndrome, a severe transformation of acute lymphoblastic/lymphocytic leukemia (ALL) characterized by the proliferation of blasts into large, abnormal lymphocytes. The evidence suggests that Ofatumumab may be repurposed for this condition, as it is indicated in conjunction with other drugs such as Idelalisib and Duvelisib, which are also used in the management of ALL. These drugs are known to target pathways involved in leukemia progression, including the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling and B-cell receptor pathways. The shared indication for Richter Syndrome and the downstream indication for ALL indicates a potential mechanistic link, where Ofatumumab may exert its effect through a mechanism that influences these pathways, potentially leading to the transformation of leukemic cells. The integrated evidence suggests that Ofatumumab could be a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of ALL, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted to confirm its role in the disease process.",
